DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ldpd(8) daemon implements the Label Distribution Protocol as de‐
     scribed in RFC 5036.

     The llddppdd..ccoonnff config file is divided into the following main sections:

     _M_a_c_r_o_s
           Definitions of variables that can be used later, simplifying the
           configuration file.

     _G_l_o_b_a_l _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Global settings for ldpd(8).

     _A_d_d_r_e_s_s_-_f_a_m_i_l_y _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Address-family specific parameters.

     _I_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_s _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Interface-specific parameters.

     _T_a_r_g_e_t_e_d _n_e_i_g_h_b_o_r_s _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Targeted neighbor specific parameters.

     _N_e_i_g_h_b_o_r_s _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Neighbor-specific parameters.

     _L_a_y_e_r _2 _V_P_N_s _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Layer 2 VPNs parameters as per RFC 4447.

     Argument names not beginning with a letter, digit, or underscore must be
     quoted.

     Additional configuration files can be included with the iinncclluuddee keyword,
     for example:

           include "/etc/ldpd.sub.conf"

   MMaaccrrooss
     A macro is defined with a command of the form _n_a_m_e=_v_a_l_u_e.  The macro _n_a_m_e
     can contain letters, digits, and underscores and cannot be a reserved
     word (for example, nneeiigghhbboorr).  Within unquoted arguments, the string
     $_n_a_m_e is later expanded to _v_a_l_u_e.

   GGlloobbaall ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     Several settings can be configured globally or within a more restricted
     scope, like per address-family or per interface.  The only settings that
     can be set globally and not overruled are listed below.

     ddss--cciissccoo--iinntteerroopp (yyeess|nnoo)
             If set to yyeess, Cisco non-compliant format will be used to send
             and interpret the Dual-Stack capability TLV.  The default is nnoo.

     ffiibb--uuppddaattee (yyeess|nnoo)
             If set to nnoo, do not update the Label Forwarding Information
             Base, a.k.a. the kernel routing table.  The default is yyeess.

     rrddoommaaiinn _t_a_b_l_e_i_d
             Specifies the routing table ldpd(8) should modify.  Table 0 is
             the default table.

     rroouutteerr--iidd _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
             Set the router ID; in combination with labelspace it forms the
             LSR-ID.  If not specified, the numerically lowest IP address of
             the router will be used.

     ttccpp mmdd55ssiigg ppaasssswwoorrdd _s_e_c_r_e_t [_l_s_r_-_i_d[/_p_r_e_f_i_x]]
     ttccpp mmdd55ssiigg kkeeyy _s_e_c_r_e_t [_l_s_r_-_i_d[/_p_r_e_f_i_x]]
     nnoo ttccpp mmdd55ssiigg [_l_s_r_-_i_d[/_p_r_e_f_i_x]]
             Enable or disable TCP MD5 signatures per RFC 5036.  The shared
             secret can either be given as a password or hexadecimal key.  An
             optional prefix may be specified to scope the key configuration
             to a set of neighbors with the specified LSR-IDs.

                   tcp md5sig password mekmitasdigoat 192.168.0.0/24
                   no tcp md5sig 192.168.0.25

     ttrraannssppoorrtt--pprreeffeerreennccee (iippvv44|iippvv66)
             Specify the preferred address-family for TCP transport connec‐
             tions.  If two dual-stack LSRs preferences does not match, no LDP
             session will be established.  The default is iippvv66.

   AAddddrreessss--ffaammiillyy ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     Each address-family can have several parameters configured individually,
     otherwise they are inherited.

           address-family ipv6 {
                   explicit-null yes
                   transport-address 2001:db8::50
                   interface em0
           }

     eexxpplliicciitt--nnuullll (yyeess|nnoo)
             If set to yyeess, advertise explicit-null labels in place of im‐
             plicit-null labels for directly connected prefixes.  The default
             is nnoo.

     ggttssmm--eennaabbllee (yyeess|nnoo)
             If set to yyeess, ldpd(8) will use the GTSM procedures described in
             RFC 6720 (for the IPv4 address-family) and RFC 7552 (for the IPv6
             address-family).

             Since GTSM is mandatory for LDPv6, the only effect of disabling
             GTSM for the IPv6 address-family is that ldpd(8) will not discard
             packets with a hop limit below 255.  This may be necessary to in‐
             teroperate with older implementations.  Outgoing packets will
             still be sent using a hop limit of 255 for maximum compatibility.

             If GTSM is enabled, multi-hop neighbors should have either GTSM
             disabled individually or configured with an appropriate gtsm-hops
             distance.  The default is yyeess.

     kkeeeeppaalliivvee _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Set the keepalive timeout in seconds.  The default value is 180;
             valid range is 3-65535.

     ttaarrggeetteedd--hheelllloo--aacccceepptt (yyeess|nnoo)
             If set to yyeess, allow LDP sessions to be established with remote
             neighbors that have not been specifically configured.  The de‐
             fault is nnoo.

     ttrraannssppoorrtt--aaddddrreessss _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
             Set the local address to be used in the TCP sessions.  For the
             IPv4 address-family, the router-id will be used if this option is
             not specified.  For the IPv6 address-family, this option must be
             specified.

   IInntteerrffaacceess ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     Each interface can have several parameters configured individually, oth‐
     erwise they are inherited.

           address-family ipv4 {
                   interface em0 {
                           link-hello-holdtime 9
                           link-hello-interval 3
                   }
           }

     Interface-specific parameters are listed below.

     lliinnkk--hheelllloo--hhoollddttiimmee _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Set the hello holdtime in seconds.  The maximum time ldpd(8) will
             wait between two consecutive hello messages from a peer before it
             is marked as being down.  The default value is 15; valid range is
             3-65535.

     lliinnkk--hheelllloo--iinntteerrvvaall _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Set the hello interval in seconds.  The default value is 5; valid
             range is 1-65535.

   TTaarrggeetteedd nneeiigghhbboorrss ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     Each targeted neighbor can have several parameters configured individu‐
     ally, otherwise they are inherited.

           address-family ipv4 {
                   targeted-neighbor A.B.C.D {
                           targeted-hello-holdtime 90
                           targeted-hello-interval 10
                   }
           }
           address-family ipv6 {
                   targeted-neighbor 2001:db8::1
           }

     Targeted-neighbor specific parameters are listed below.

     ttaarrggeetteedd--hheelllloo--hhoollddttiimmee _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Set the hello holdtime in seconds.  The maximum time ldpd(8) will
             wait between two consecutive hello messages from a peer before it
             is marked as being down.  The default value is 45.

     ttaarrggeetteedd--hheelllloo--iinntteerrvvaall _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Set the hello interval in seconds.  The default value is 5; valid
             range is 1-65535.

   NNeeiigghhbboorrss ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     The nneeiigghhbboorr section allows for the configuration of neighbor-specific
     parameters.  Note, however, that ldpd(8) uses the hello discovery mecha‐
     nism to discover its neighbors.  Without an underlying adjacency these
     commands have no effect.  A neighbor is identified by its LSR-ID, not by
     its remote address.  The neighbor-specific parameters apply for both LD‐
     PoIPv4 and LDPoIPv6 sessions.

           neighbor A.B.C.D {
           }

     Neighbor-specific parameters are listed below.

     kkeeeeppaalliivvee _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Set the keepalive timeout in seconds.  Inherited from the global
             configuration if not given.  Valid range is 3-65535.

     ggttssmm--eennaabbllee (yyeess|nnoo)
             Override the inherited configuration and enable/disable GTSM for
             this neighbor.

     ggttssmm--hhooppss _h_o_p_s
             Set the maximum number of hops the neighbor may be away.  When
             GTSM is enabled for this neighbor, incoming packets are required
             to have a TTL/hop limit of 256 minus this value, ensuring they
             have not passed through more than the expected number of hops.
             The default value is 1; valid range is 1-255.

     ttccpp mmdd55ssiigg ppaasssswwoorrdd _s_e_c_r_e_t
             Enable TCP MD5 signatures per RFC 5036 with the specified pass‐
             word.

     ttccpp mmdd55ssiigg kkeeyy _s_e_c_r_e_t
             Enable TCP MD5 signatures per RFC 5036 with the specified hexa‐
             decimal key.

     nnoo ttccpp mmdd55ssiigg
             Disable the use of TCP MD5 signatures.

   LLaayyeerr 22 VVPPNNss ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     ldpd(8) implements the signaling of pseudowires which can be used to im‐
     plement either the VPWS solution (also known as PWE3) or the VPLS solu‐
     tion.  Currently only the VPLS solution is supported.

           l2vpn name type vpls {
                   bridge bridge0
                   interface em1
                   pseudowire mpw1 {
                           pw-id 100
                           neighbor-id 192.168.1.10
                   }
                   pseudowire mpw2 {
                           pw-id 200
                           neighbor-id 10.0.1.5
                   }
           }

     Layer 2 VPN specific parameters are listed below.

     bbrriiddggee _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e
             Set the bridge interface the VPLS is associated with.  This pa‐
             rameter is optional and is only used to remove MAC addresses re‐
             ceived from MAC address withdrawal messages.  Only one bridge in‐
             terface can be set.

     iinntteerrffaaccee _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e
             Configure a non pseudowire interface pertaining to the VPLS.
             This parameter is optional and is only used to send MAC address
             withdrawal messages when the specified interface is shutdown.
             Multiple interfaces can be configured.

     mmttuu _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Set the MTU advertised in the pseudowires.  Local and remote MTUs
             must match for a pseudowire to be set up.  The default value is
             1500.

     ttyyppee (eetthheerrnneett|eetthheerrnneett--ttaaggggeedd)
             Specify the type of the configured pseudowires.  The type must be
             the same at both endpoints.  The default is eetthheerrnneett.

PPSSEEUUDDOOWWIIRREESS
     Each mpw(4) pseudowire interface can have several parameters configured
     individually, otherwise they are inherited.  A pseudowire interface is
     specified by its name.

           pseudowire mpw5 {
                   pw-id 5000
                   neighbor-id 172.16.1.50
           }

     Pseudowire-specific parameters are listed below.

     ccoonnttrrooll--wwoorrdd (yyeess|nnoo)
             Specify whether the use of the control word is preferred or not
             preferred.  The default is yyeess.

     nneeiigghhbboorr--aaddddrr _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
             Specify the IPv4 or IPv6 address of the remote endpoint of the
             pseudowire.  A targeted neighbor will automatically be created
             for this address.  By default, the LSR-ID of the remote endpoint
             of the pseudowire will be used.

     nneeiigghhbboorr--iidd _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
             Specify the LSR-ID of the remote endpoint of the pseudowire.

     ppww--iidd _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Set the PW ID used to identify the pseudowire.  The PW ID must be
             the same at both endpoints.  Valid range is 1-4294967295.

     ssttaattuuss--ttllvv (yyeess|nnoo)
             Specify whether the use of the Status TLV is preferred or not
             preferred.  The default is yyeess.

